WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.180
Multiplying the Fourier matrix by a vector

2
00:00:06.258 --> 00:00:08.757
costs n squared multiplications

3
00:00:08.835 --> 00:00:11.256
every entry of the matrix meets

4
00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:13.130
an entry of the vector.

5
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.332
For a million samples, about a trillion.

6
00:00:16.332 --> 00:00:19.378
An audio file has millions of samples

7
00:00:19.378 --> 00:00:20.940
an image even more.

8
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.442
How much could a clever method save?

9
00:00:24.442 --> 00:00:25.930
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.972
One idea, used again and again

11
00:00:31.972 --> 00:00:35.545
split the input into even and odd positions.

12
00:00:35.545 --> 00:00:38.404
Here are the eighth roots of unity

13
00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:41.263
the powers of w for n equals eight.

14
00:00:41.263 --> 00:00:44.678
The even powers land on every other point

15
00:00:44.678 --> 00:00:47.457
they are exactly the fourth roots

16
00:00:47.457 --> 00:00:49.443
the powers of w squared.

17
00:00:49.522 --> 00:00:52.222
So the even entries form a Fourier

18
00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:54.446
transform of half the size

19
00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:56.590
and so do the odd entries.

20
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.854
Output k is e k plus w to the k times o k.

21
00:01:01.965 --> 00:01:05.422
Output k plus n over two is e k

22
00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:08.545
minus w to the k times o k

23
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:12.113
because w to the power k plus n

24
00:01:12.225 --> 00:01:15.459
over two is minus w to the k

25
00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:19.250
the opposite point on the circle.

26
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.005
So one product, w to the k times o k

27
00:01:23.005 --> 00:01:24.386
is used twice

28
00:01:24.478 --> 00:01:27.699
added for one output and subtracted

29
00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:29.080
for the other.

30
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.726
For n equals four: two two-point transforms

31
00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:37.423
of x zero, x two and of x one, x three

32
00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:40.380
then four sums and differences.

33
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.384
One transform of size n costs

34
00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:45.581
two of size n over two

35
00:01:45.581 --> 00:01:48.601
plus n over two multiplications.

36
00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:52.170
Repeat until the pieces have size one

37
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.092
log two of n levels

38
00:01:54.092 --> 00:01:57.570
each with n over two multiplications.

39
00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.626
For eight samples

40
00:01:59.708 --> 00:02:02.168
twelve multiplications instead

41
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.397
of sixty-four.

42
00:02:03.397 --> 00:02:05.857
For one thousand twenty-four

43
00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:08.563
five thousand one hundred twenty

44
00:02:08.645 --> 00:02:10.858
instead of about a million

45
00:02:10.858 --> 00:02:12.990
two hundred times faster.

46
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.071
For a million samples

47
00:02:15.071 --> 00:02:18.138
about a hundred thousand times faster

48
00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:21.363
a fraction of a second instead of hours.

49
00:02:21.363 --> 00:02:24.195
This is the fast Fourier transform

50
00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:27.184
usually credited to Cooley and Tukey

51
00:02:27.184 --> 00:02:29.780
and known much earlier to Gauss.

52
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.003
So fast must mean approximate.

53
00:02:33.091 --> 00:02:35.910
Is the FFT a shortcut that gives

54
00:02:35.998 --> 00:02:38.200
roughly the right answer?

55
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.005
No.

56
00:02:39.005 --> 00:02:42.006
A short recursive version of the split

57
00:02:42.006 --> 00:02:44.032
about ten lines of Python

58
00:02:44.107 --> 00:02:46.207
agrees with NumPy's f f t on

59
00:02:46.282 --> 00:02:49.433
a thousand random samples, up to rounding.

60
00:02:49.508 --> 00:02:51.684
It is the same multiplication

61
00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:53.409
by the Fourier matrix

62
00:02:53.484 --> 00:02:55.360
organized so that most of

63
00:02:55.435 --> 00:02:56.860
the work is shared.

64
00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.390
Where does this matter?

65
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:00.746
In convolution.

66
00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:03.119
Blur a row of eight pixels

67
00:03:03.204 --> 00:03:06.340
each new value is half the pixel plus

68
00:03:06.425 --> 00:03:08.798
a quarter of each neighbour

69
00:03:08.798 --> 00:03:12.612
and the last pixel's neighbour is the first.

70
00:03:12.612 --> 00:03:16.002
A bright bar, zero, zero, eight, eight

71
00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:19.732
eight, zero, zero, zero, becomes zero, two

72
00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:22.613
six, eight, six, two, zero, zero

73
00:03:22.613 --> 00:03:23.800
softer edges.

74
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.178
The matrix of this blur is constant

75
00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:29.687
along each wrapped diagonal

76
00:03:29.687 --> 00:03:31.417
a circulant matrix.

77
00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:35.310
Multiplying by it is a circular convolution.

78
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:37.672
Here is the key fact.

79
00:03:37.756 --> 00:03:40.895
Every column of the Fourier matrix is

80
00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.865
an eigenvector of every circulant.

81
00:03:43.865 --> 00:03:47.513
Take column one for n equals four: one, i

82
00:03:47.513 --> 00:03:49.209
minus one, minus i.

83
00:03:49.294 --> 00:03:52.603
Shifting it by one place only turns all

84
00:03:52.687 --> 00:03:55.826
its entries by the same quarter turn

85
00:03:55.826 --> 00:03:59.050
and a circulant is built from shifts.

86
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.820
So the eigenvalues are the f

87
00:04:01.906 --> 00:04:03.980
f t of the first column.

88
00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.091
For the blur: one for the constant

89
00:04:07.091 --> 00:04:10.115
then point eight five, point five

90
00:04:10.115 --> 00:04:11.498
point one five

91
00:04:11.498 --> 00:04:14.781
and zero for the fastest alternation.

92
00:04:14.781 --> 00:04:17.201
So C is F lambda F inverse

93
00:04:17.201 --> 00:04:19.620
the blur keeps the average

94
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.694
as in Lecture eighteen

95
00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.200
and kills the finest detail.

96
00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.684
That gives the convolution rule.

97
00:04:27.684 --> 00:04:31.132
To convolve c with x: transform both

98
00:04:31.132 --> 00:04:34.852
multiply entry by entry, transform back.

99
00:04:34.852 --> 00:04:38.300
Three FFTs instead of n squared work.

100
00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.009
Multiplying polynomials is a convolution

101
00:04:42.093 --> 00:04:43.940
of their coefficients.

102
00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.046
One plus two t plus three t squared

103
00:04:47.046 --> 00:04:50.657
times four plus five t, comes out as four

104
00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:53.259
thirteen, twenty-two, fifteen.

105
00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.442
But pad with zeros first.

106
00:04:55.442 --> 00:04:57.038
With length three

107
00:04:57.122 --> 00:04:59.305
the last coefficient wraps

108
00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:03.083
around onto the first, and you get nineteen

109
00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:04.930
thirteen, twenty-two.

110
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.104
Convolutional networks slide small

111
00:05:08.187 --> 00:05:10.014
filters across images

112
00:05:10.097 --> 00:05:14.084
and long convolutions in audio and some sequence

113
00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:17.240
models are computed exactly this way.

114
00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:18.677
To sum up.

115
00:05:18.677 --> 00:05:21.247
The FFT splits even from odd

116
00:05:21.247 --> 00:05:23.645
reusing each product twice

117
00:05:23.731 --> 00:05:26.900
and computes exactly the same Fourier

118
00:05:26.986 --> 00:05:29.299
transform in n log n steps.

119
00:05:29.385 --> 00:05:32.126
Fourier vectors are eigenvectors

120
00:05:32.212 --> 00:05:33.839
of every circulant

121
00:05:33.839 --> 00:05:37.180
so convolution becomes multiplication.

122
00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.848
Next: a matrix depends on the basis.

123
00:05:40.848 --> 00:05:42.392
Change the basis

124
00:05:42.392 --> 00:05:45.480
and the same map gets a new matrix.
